-- Avik (14:36 UTC): Vaughan Pratt for his life's work: the mathematical content here is what he and the
-- Chu spaces group at Stanford look for. The text and its index are a Chu space: occurrences (points,
-- time) paired with the sequences they instantiate (states, information), Pratt's duality of time and
-- information; his higher-dimensional automata (1991) are cubical sets of concurrent events.
-- Built 2026-11-17 (PR #10, item 3): lib/repeats.cub, each property proved for every text:
-- tokenize-content (the tokens joined are the text without its spaces; a token is its bytes, identity
-- by content); word-order, lex-order, pair-order (the orders are total); suffix-array-sorted and
-- suffix-array-permutation; lcp-array-ok (each value a common prefix, lcp-common, that can go no
-- further, lcp-stops); earlier-match-max and earlier-match-at (the longest earlier match is maximal
-- and attained at an earlier point); equivalent (an equivalence relation, given by class
-- representatives) and index-upto (the index up to it). The suffix array is merge-sorted, comparing
-- suffixes token by token, and the LCP is taken pair by pair (prefix doubling and Kasai are the passes
-- below). Measured with experiments/repeats/run.cub in this container (2 cores, about 3.2 GB for the
-- process): the first 10 modules by path, 140,836 tokens: read and tokenized 7.8 s, suffix and LCP
-- arrays 217 s, 130,031 positions in a repeat; the first 40, 407,418 tokens: 26 s, 624 s, 384,944 in
-- a repeat, peak memory 1.94 GB. The whole source did not run: reading and tokenizing alone held 1.35
-- GB at 150 of the 326 modules (972,121 tokens) and was killed past 2.5 GB before 250; the run over
-- all of it was killed for memory at 35 s. The cause, measured: the evaluator holds the text, its
-- tokens and the sorted entries as cons cells, about 4.8 KB per token at peak, and the sort takes
-- about 1.5 ms per token, each comparison walking the common prefix (over 92% of these tokens are in
-- a repeat).
-- Second pass (2026-11-17, PR #10): prefix doubling is built and proved, lib/repeats-fast.cub. ranks t
-- ranks every suffix by its first token, then by the pair (its rank, the rank 2^k further on), each
-- rank one more than the count of smaller keys, read from a search tree over the sorted keys; ranks-ok:
-- the final ranks compare as the whole suffixes; suffix-array-fast-is: the array sorted by them is
-- suffix-array t (sorted-unique). Measured with experiments/repeats/run-fast.cub against run.cub, peak
-- resident memory sampled: 1 module, 767 tokens: 2.6 s against 0.33 s for both arrays by the merge
-- sort; 2 modules, 57,637 tokens: the merge sort and pairwise LCP 52.7 s at 409 MB peak, prefix
-- doubling killed past 3 GB before it ended; 4 modules, 95,317 tokens: 95.9 s at 572 MB, doubling
-- killed past 3 GB. So in this evaluator the doubling, as written, costs far more memory per token
-- than the merge sort it replaces; where that memory goes (whether the per-round search tree and rank
-- list are shared across the per-suffix lookups) is not yet measured.
-- Kasai's LCP, 2026-11-17 (PR #10, item 3), lib/repeats-kasai.cub, over
-- any rank list that orders the suffixes (FinalP): kasai-by walks the suffixes in text order, finds each
-- one's predecessor by rank in the search tree (k-tree: the tree's query is the sorted list's last
-- element below the key) and computes its common prefix from the last one's less one (k-skip: from a
-- lower bound the rest compared is the common prefix); Kasai's invariant is k-kasai (from k-btw: between
-- a and c in the order, b shares with c at least what a shares); kasai-by-ok: every entry (r, p, s, l)
-- has l = lcp p s, p the greatest suffix below s (its neighbour in the suffix array), so each l is the
-- common prefix that can go no further (kasai-lcp-at, from lcp-common and lcp-stops); kasai-ok over the
-- prefix-doubling ranks, kasai-ms-ok over the merge-sorted key tree's ranks; kasai-example and
-- kasai-example-pd checked by computation ("a b a b a": 3, 2, 1, 0). The existing theorem names stand.
-- Measured with experiments/repeats/run-kasai.cub (the merge-sorted key tree's ranks, then Kasai, then
-- the same pairs' common prefixes compared from the start), first N modules by path, this container:
-- N=1, 767 tokens: the key tree built in 274 ms; the ranks of all suffixes (767 queries into it) 14.7 s;
-- Kasai 259 ms; the same pairs compared from the start 22 ms; both sums 1198; peak 193 MB. N=2, 57,637
-- tokens: tokenized 2.6 s; the ranks unfinished at 1,700 s, 425 MB after tokenizing, 635 MB at 1,700 s.
-- N=10, 141,994 tokens: tokenized 6.1 s; the ranks unfinished at 1,700 s, peak 1.08 GB; N=40 not run.
-- Memory per token, from these runs: the library and checker loaded,
-- about 190 MB whatever the text; the text read and tokenized, about 4.1 KB per token ((425 - 190) MB
-- over 57,637); the ranks, about 3.6 KB more per token by 1,700 s and still growing; Kasai's output was
-- not reached past N=1. The cost is in the evaluator's representation (src/core.h, src/net.c): a value is
-- a net of cells, each cell 48 bytes of header (kind, port count, flags, a word, a payload pointer, its
-- memo key, a pending entry) and 8 bytes per port; a wire not yet joined is a 24-byte variable (port,
-- next, key); a value read by more than one reader is shared through duplicators (O_DUP), each part that
-- has parts held once in a malloc'd box with a reference cell on each side (net.c, the copy of a
-- value's top). The search tree and the suffix lists every query compares are read that way, by each of
-- the n queries: the 767 queries cost 14.7 s, 19 ms each, though their common prefixes average 1.5
-- tokens (the sum 1198 over 767), while the tree itself was built once in 274 ms. So the reads of shared
-- structure, not the length of the comparisons and not the LCP, take the time at every size measured;
-- Kasai's carried bound saves comparisons where common prefixes are long, and at N=1, where they are
-- short, comparing the same pairs from the start is faster (22 ms against 259 ms, the difference the
-- predecessor queries).
